  + 
  + ## Multi-source synthesis
  + 
  + When a research question touches 3+ sources or any single source exceeds 5KB, the standard single-source approach (curl → read → summarize in main context) breaks down: raw content bloats your context window, cross-source reasoning gets noisy, and you lose the thread. Use the RLM substrate (sub-agents + SharedState) to fan out, digest in parallel, and synthesize.
  + 
  + ### When to use multi-source vs. single-source
  + 
  + - **1–2 sources, all under 5KB each**: Use the existing single-source procedure above. Sub-agent overhead isn't worth it.
  + - **3+ sources on a single topic** (papers, blog posts, docs, discussions): Use multi-source synthesis.
  + - **Any single source exceeding 5KB**: Use multi-source synthesis (even if it's only one source — the sub-agent digests it so your main context stays clean).
  + 
  + ### Procedure
  + 
  + #### 1. Fetch and store each source
  + 
  + Fetch each source via `bash` (curl) and store it in shared state. Use the namespace convention `research.<topic>.source-<N>`:
  + 
